From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jasmine <jasmine@localhost>
Date: Wed, 5 Nov 2026 01:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] jasmine-j0134: present or complete a swap whose layer is
 another client's surface

netlog-swap133 shows the j0133 recovery itself working twice (swaps 364 and
369 retired) and then a freeze in synchronous mode: swapWait for 377 times out
forever while swaps 378/379 were begun and ended with two layers.  Layer 0 of
379 was surface 650, pid 302 (Maps' CAMetalLayer drawable); layer 1 was
surface 3, pid 57 (backboardd).  Both records are copy-path frames.

Queue mode (QUEUE FALLBACK reason 1).  A swap that cannot get the present
scope for 200 ms is rejected with reason 1 and rejectFrame() falls the whole
display back to synchronous mode for the rest of the boot.  The present scope
is held by queuePresent() for its entire admission wait: up to 1000 ms for a
frame with a render ticket (or 100 ms without), plus waitLatch().  A single
slow present therefore exceeds the 200 ms swap bound, and what should be one
dropped frame becomes a permanent mode change.

Synchronous mode (the freeze).  After the fallback there are no more serials
(queueFallback sets fQueueActive = false; SwapBegin then only bumps fSwapID),
so j0133's recovery cannot fire (it requires fQueueActive and serial).  In the
copy path swap() validates every held layer with
sweet_surface_cpu_access(surface, true), which waits up to 100 ms on the
surface's render ticket (j0110's sweet_ticket_wait), and then waits up to
100 ms on every layer's gpuSeqno.  On a ticket timeout the old code returned
kIOReturnSuccess without calling present() or noteShown(), so fEndID stayed
377.  A page's SwapWait with sweetfb_swapwait=1 is done only once fEndID is
greater than id (mode 2 uses fShownID; id - fSwapID is negative for 378/379,
and the queue rule is skipped), so nothing newer is ever programmed and CA's
display thread polls can_update forever: the circular freeze.  The layer whose
ticket does not become ready is not necessarily backboardd's: a drawable that
Maps still produces has a ticket opened by Maps' encode/replay, and the
display must not serialize on another process's generation.  The ticket stats
bracket the event: netlog-swap133.txt:11144 has live/high 2/3 and timeout 1;
:11196 has timeout 4 and live 0, so open tickets existed and three display
ticket waits timed out across the fallback and the 378/379 copies.

j0134 (default on, boot-arg sweetfb_clientlayer=0 restores j0133 behaviour):

- Track the SwapEnd caller's pid in the user client and pass the client to
  swap().  A layer whose creatorPID is a different process is a client layer
  (Maps' drawable inside backboardd's swap).  CoreAnimation has already waited
  for the app's present before putting it in the swap; the display client's
  ticket wait cannot validate it, and waiting can only consume the display's
  bound.  Such a layer is treated as ready (cache access only, no ticket and
  no gpuSeqno wait); the swap client's own surfaces keep the existing ticket
  validation.
- A ticket timeout for the swap client's own surface no longer returns
  silently.  In queue mode the frame is dropped and the queue survives; in
  synchronous mode the swap is completed by noteShown() (or, if the MDP still
  scans a client surface, by the sync watchdog below, after copying it), so
  fEndID never lags a successful SwapEnd.  (The panel keeps the previous
  frame; this is the "mark shown" completion, not a torn copy.)
- A present-scope timeout in queue mode drops the frame and keeps the queue;
  eight consecutive drops still fall back (reason 1), so a genuinely stuck
  presenter is still retired.  In synchronous mode the swap retries once and
  then completes by noteShown() instead of being lost.
- Extend j0133's watchdog to synchronous mode: when the waited swap is the
  one on the panel (fEndID == fShownID == id), newer swaps were begun, nothing
  is in flight, and 50 ms passed, complete the wait.  Queue mode keeps j0133's
  existing serial/token rule.  If the MDP still scans a client surface, copy
  it first with abandonShown() as j0133 does.

Bounded logging (8 lines a boot, first events only) names the client layer and
the completion/drop decision.  No build or boot has been done for this patch.

---
 .../iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetFramebuffer.cpp          | 204 ++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 198 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetFramebuffer.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetFramebuffer.cpp
index 534d021..9f228c5 100644
--- a/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetFramebuffer.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetFramebuffer.cpp
@@ -47,6 +47,7 @@ extern "C" void sweet_fb_submit_fault(uint32_t reason);
 #include <pexpert/device_tree.h>
 #include <mach/vm_param.h>
 #include <kern/clock.h>
+#include <kern/task.h>
 #include <kern/thread.h>
 #include <kern/sched_prim.h>
 
@@ -171,6 +172,23 @@ sweet_fb_abandon(void)
 {
 	return sweet_fb_abandon_on;
 }
+/*
+ * j0134 (default on, boot-arg sweetfb_clientlayer=0 off): CoreAnimation also puts a
+ * client's own surface into the swap as a layer (Maps' full-screen CAMetalLayer drawable
+ * next to backboardd's composited surface).  That surface was made by another process,
+ * its producer is the app's own command buffer, and CoreAnimation already waited for the
+ * app's present before handing the drawable over; the display client's render tickets
+ * only cover its own surfaces.  Waiting for such a layer's ticket can only time out, and
+ * a synchronous SwapEnd that timed out used to return success without presenting or
+ * advancing fEndID, so the next SwapWait could never complete (sync-mode freeze).
+ */
+static bool sweet_fb_client_layer_on = true;
+static bool
+sweet_fb_clientlayer(void)
+{
+	return sweet_fb_client_layer_on;
+}
+static uint32_t sweet_fb_clientlayer_logs;      /* bounded: 8 lines a boot */
 static uint64_t sweet_fb_slept_us, sweet_fb_spun_us;
 
 /*
@@ -434,12 +452,14 @@ public:
 	void          setPanelLevel(uint32_t level);
 	void addClient(IOMobileFramebufferUserClient *client);
 	void removeClient(IOMobileFramebufferUserClient *client);
-	IOReturn swap(const uint8_t *rec);
+	IOReturn swap(const uint8_t *rec, IOMobileFramebufferUserClient *client);
+	/* j0134: a layer surface created by a process other than the SwapEnd caller */
+	bool          clientLayer(SweetSurface *s, IOMobileFramebufferUserClient *swapClient);
 	/* Build 1: fLock protects queue, tokens, ownership and submit reservations. */
 	bool          fProtocol, fQueueActive, fPresentBusy, fQueueWaiting;
 	volatile bool fTerminal;
 	bool terminal(void) const { return __atomic_load_n(&fTerminal, __ATOMIC_ACQUIRE); }
-	uint32_t      fEpoch, fWriters, fFallbacks, fDrops, fAdmissions, fLatchRetries, fLatchTimeoutChecks;
+	uint32_t      fEpoch, fWriters, fFallbacks, fDrops, fScopeDrops, fAdmissions, fLatchRetries, fLatchTimeoutChecks;
 	uint32_t      fGuard[5], fGuardReject[5], fDropReason[16];
 	uint64_t      fGuardUS[5];
 	SweetSurface *fFaultHold[4], *fSyncHold[4];
@@ -515,6 +535,7 @@ class IOMobileFramebufferUserClient : public IOUserClient {
 public:
 	AppleCLCD          *fFB;
 	task_t              fTask;
+	int                 fPid;                  /* j0134: SwapEnd caller, for client-layer attribution */
 	uint64_t            fNewestSerial;
 	uint32_t            fSwapWaitTimeouts;     /* j0132: consecutive SwapWait timeouts */
 	bool                fClosed;
@@ -776,6 +797,9 @@ AppleCLCD::start(IOService *provider)
 	uint32_t abandon = 1;
 	PE_parse_boot_argn("sweetfb_abandon", &abandon, sizeof(abandon));
 	sweet_fb_abandon_on = abandon != 0;
+	uint32_t clientlayer = 1;
+	PE_parse_boot_argn("sweetfb_clientlayer", &clientlayer, sizeof(clientlayer));
+	sweet_fb_client_layer_on = clientlayer != 0;
 	if (queue && (!sleep || topaz || !fScaler || console || !fFlipVA[0] || !fFlipVA[1] || sweet_fb_nohold())) {
 		IOLog("SWEET FB: queue refused: requires sleep, jasmine scaler, flip buffers, holds and GUI\n");
 		queue = 0;
@@ -785,6 +809,7 @@ AppleCLCD::start(IOService *provider)
 	fEpoch = 1;
 	fScanOwned = false;
 	fShownAt = 0;
+	fScopeDrops = 0;
 	PE_parse_boot_argn("sweetgpu_tickets", &sweet_tickets_on, sizeof(sweet_tickets_on));
 	if (sweet_tickets_on && !fProtocol) {
 		IOLog("SWEET GPU: tickets refused: requires sweetfb_queue=1\n");
@@ -1657,8 +1682,27 @@ sweet_fps_count(void)
 	}
 }
 
+/*
+ * j0134: true for a layer surface that the SwapEnd caller did not create (Maps' own
+ * CAMetalLayer drawable inside backboardd's swap).  Its producer is the app's command
+ * buffer; CoreAnimation hands the drawable over only after the app's present, and the
+ * kernel render tickets are opened/retired by the writer process itself, not by the
+ * display client, so a ticket wait here would wait for a generation that cannot arrive.
+ * creatorPID is the creator, not necessarily the current holder; that is what we want:
+ * a surface the display client also writes is scanned/copied through its own path.
+ */
+bool
+AppleCLCD::clientLayer(SweetSurface *s, IOMobileFramebufferUserClient *swapClient)
+{
+	if (!sweet_fb_clientlayer() || s == NULL || swapClient == NULL || s->creatorPID == 0) {
+		return false;
+	}
+	int pid = swapClient->fPid;
+	return pid > 0 && s->creatorPID != pid;
+}
+
 IOReturn
-AppleCLCD::swap(const uint8_t *rec)
+AppleCLCD::swap(const uint8_t *rec, IOMobileFramebufferUserClient *client)
 {
 	sweet_fps_count();
 	sweet_ddrgov_activity(4, 0);            /* a present keeps the DDR governor at the full rate */
@@ -1733,11 +1777,61 @@ AppleCLCD::swap(const uint8_t *rec)
 					return kIOReturnAborted;
 				}
 				for (unsigned i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
-					if (sweet_tickets_enabled() && held[i] && sweet_surface_cpu_access(held[i], true) != kIOReturnSuccess) {
+					if (held[i] == NULL || !sweet_tickets_enabled()) {
+						continue;
+					}
+					bool own = !clientLayer(held[i], client);
+					if (!own) {
+						/*
+						 * j0134: a surface created by another process (Maps'
+						 * CAMetalLayer drawable) has no render ticket the
+						 * display client can wait for: CoreAnimation already
+						 * waited for the app's present before putting it in
+						 * this swap. Treat it as ready; never wait for a
+						 * generation that cannot arrive.
+						 */
+						if (sweet_fb_clientlayer() && sweet_fb_clientlayer_logs < 8) {
+							sweet_fb_clientlayer_logs++;
+							IOLog("SWEET FB: swap %u layer %u surface %u pid %d: client layer, no ticket wait\n",
+							    get32(rec, 0x50), i, held[i]->id, held[i]->creatorPID);
+						}
+						(void)sweet_surface_cpu_access(held[i], false);
+						continue;
+					}
+					if (sweet_surface_cpu_access(held[i], true) == kIOReturnSuccess) {
+						continue;
+					}
+					if (!sweet_fb_clientlayer()) {
+						/* j0133 behaviour, kept for the switch: silent drop. */
 						rejectFrame(get32(rec, 0x50), 16, kIOReturnAborted);
 						sweet_hold_set(held, false);
 						return kIOReturnSuccess;
 					}
+					/*
+					 * j0134: never drop a SwapEnd without completing it. In queue
+					 * mode the latch rule retires this frame; after a fallback
+					 * (synchronous mode) there is no later latch, so mark it shown
+					 * or every later SwapWait for it can never complete.
+					 */
+					rejectFrame(get32(rec, 0x50), 16, kIOReturnAborted);
+					IOLockLock(fLock);
+					bool sync = !fQueueActive;
+					/* If the MDP still fetches a client surface, leave the completion to
+					 * swapWait's sync recovery, which copies that frame into our buffer
+					 * first (abandonShown); noteShown alone would let CA reuse it. */
+					bool complete = sync && (fScanOwned || !fShownValid);
+					IOLockUnlock(fLock);
+					if (complete) {
+						noteShown(get32(rec, 0x50));
+					}
+					if (sweet_fb_clientlayer_logs < 8) {
+						sweet_fb_clientlayer_logs++;
+						IOLog("SWEET FB: swap %u layer %u surface %u pid %d: ticket not ready, %s\n",
+						    get32(rec, 0x50), i, held[i]->id, held[i]->creatorPID,
+						    !sync ? "dropped (queue)" : complete ? "completed (sync)" : "sync recovery");
+					}
+					sweet_hold_set(held, false);
+					return kIOReturnSuccess;
 				}
 				uint64_t until;
 				clock_interval_to_deadline(100, kMillisecondScale, &until);
@@ -1759,6 +1854,58 @@ AppleCLCD::swap(const uint8_t *rec)
 			IOLockUnlock(fLock);
 			absolutetime_to_nanoseconds(mach_absolute_time() - start, &ns);
 			if (ns >= 200 * NSEC_PER_MSEC) {
+				bool queueNow = false, fallback = false;
+				uint32_t drops = 0;
+				if (sweet_fb_clientlayer()) {
+					IOLockLock(fLock);
+					queueNow = fQueueActive;
+					if (queueNow) {
+						drops = ++fScopeDrops;
+						fallback = drops >= 8;
+						if (fallback) { queueFallback(1); }
+					}
+					IOLockUnlock(fLock);
+				}
+				if (queueNow) {
+					/*
+					 * j0134: the queue presenter is bounded by its own watchdogs
+					 * (1 s for a ticket, 150 ms for a latch).  A swap that cannot
+					 * get the present scope for 200 ms is one slow frame, not a
+					 * protocol fault: falling back here turned a single slow
+					 * present into "synchronous for the rest of boot" (the
+					 * QUEUE FALLBACK reason 1 in netlog-swap133).  Drop the frame
+					 * and keep the queue; eight consecutive drops still fall back.
+					 */
+					if (drops <= 16 || drops % 8 == 0) {
+						IOLog("SWEET FB: swap %u: no present scope for %llu ms, dropped (%u%s)\n",
+						    get32(rec, 0x50), (unsigned long long)(ns / NSEC_PER_MSEC), drops,
+						    fallback ? ", queue fallback" : "");
+					}
+					sweet_hold_set(held, false);
+					return kIOReturnSuccess;
+				}
+				if (sweet_fb_clientlayer() && !queueNow) {
+					if (!fellBack) {
+						/* One retry, then complete the swap even if nothing presents. */
+						fellBack = true;
+						start = mach_absolute_time();
+						sweet_fb_pause(1000);
+						continue;
+					}
+					/*
+					 * j0134: a synchronous SwapEnd must never leave fEndID behind
+					 * it. Marking the swap shown is what a copy present would have
+					 * done for the transaction; the panel keeps the previous frame.
+					 */
+					noteShown(get32(rec, 0x50));
+					if (sweet_fb_clientlayer_logs < 8) {
+						sweet_fb_clientlayer_logs++;
+						IOLog("SWEET FB: swap %u: not presented after %llu ms, completed (sync)\n",
+						    get32(rec, 0x50), (unsigned long long)(ns / NSEC_PER_MSEC));
+					}
+					sweet_hold_set(held, false);
+					return kIOReturnSuccess;
+				}
 				if (fellBack) {
 					sweet_hold_set(held, false);    /* (the presenter is stuck: this frame is lost) */
 					return kIOReturnSuccess;
@@ -2329,6 +2476,7 @@ AppleCLCD::noteShown(uint32_t id)
 	IOLockLock(fLock);
 	fEndID = id;
 	fShownAt = mach_absolute_time();       /* j0133: watchdog for abandoned newer swaps */
+	fScopeDrops = 0;                        /* j0134: a shown frame ends a scope-drop streak */
 	if (fProtocol) {
 		for (unsigned i = 0; i < 128; i++) {
 			if (fTokens[i].owner && fTokens[i].id == id) { fProgramSerial = fTokens[i].serial; }
@@ -2562,6 +2710,24 @@ AppleCLCD::swapWait(uint32_t id, uint32_t timeout, IOMobileFramebufferUserClient
 				retire = true;
 			}
 		}
+		bool syncRetire = false;
+		if (!done && !fQueueActive && !newest && id && fSwapWait == 1 && sweet_fb_abandon() &&
+		    sweet_fb_clientlayer() && !fPowerOff && !fConsShown && fShownValid &&
+		    fEndID == id && fShownID == id && !fLatchValid && fLatchID == 0 &&
+		    !fQueueWaiting && !fPresentBusy && fSwapID != id && fShownAt != 0) {
+			/*
+			 * j0134: j0133's rule needs a queue-mode serial, so it cannot fire
+			 * after a fallback (serial 0).  A synchronous SwapEnd that could not
+			 * present used to return success without advancing fEndID; every
+			 * later SwapWait for the shown swap then polls forever.  Same bound:
+			 * the waited swap is on the panel, newer swaps were begun and nothing
+			 * is in flight, so no later latch can arrive.
+			 */
+			absolutetime_to_nanoseconds(mach_absolute_time() - fShownAt, &age);
+			if (age >= 50 * NSEC_PER_MSEC) {
+				syncRetire = true;
+			}
+		}
 		IOLockUnlock(fLock);
 		if (retire && abandonShown()) {
 			IOLockLock(fLock);
@@ -2591,6 +2757,31 @@ AppleCLCD::swapWait(uint32_t id, uint32_t timeout, IOMobileFramebufferUserClient
 			}
 			IOLockUnlock(fLock);
 		}
+		if (syncRetire) {
+			/* Sync presents copy/flip into one of our buffers (fScanOwned); if a direct
+			 * scan is still up, copy it before the client's page is declared free. */
+			if (!fScanOwned && !abandonShown()) {
+				syncRetire = false;
+			}
+		}
+		if (syncRetire) {
+			IOLockLock(fLock);
+			if (!fQueueActive && fShownValid && fEndID == id && fShownID == id &&
+			    !fLatchValid && fLatchID == 0 && !fQueueWaiting && !fPresentBusy) {
+				done = true;
+				static uint64_t syncAt;
+				static uint32_t syncLogs;
+				uint64_t now = mach_absolute_time(), gap = 0;
+				if (syncAt != 0) { absolutetime_to_nanoseconds(now - syncAt, &gap); }
+				if (syncLogs < 8 && (syncAt == 0 || gap >= NSEC_PER_SEC)) {
+					IOLog("SWEET FB: swapWait: sync mode, completing swap %u after %llu ms (shown %u, newest %u)\n",
+					    id, (unsigned long long)(age / NSEC_PER_MSEC), fShownID, fSwapID);
+					syncAt = now;
+					syncLogs++;
+				}
+			}
+			IOLockUnlock(fLock);
+		}
 		absolutetime_to_nanoseconds(mach_absolute_time() - start, &ns);
 		if (done || ns >= (uint64_t)ms * NSEC_PER_MSEC) {
 			if (ns > 100000) { sweet_bench_event(18, id, (uint32_t)(ns / 1000), done, newest); }
@@ -3214,6 +3405,7 @@ IOMobileFramebufferUserClient::initWithTask(task_t owningTask, void *securityID,
 		return false;
 	}
 	fTask = owningTask;
+	fPid = owningTask != TASK_NULL ? task_pid(owningTask) : 0;
 	fSwapWaitTimeouts = 0;
 	return true;
 }
@@ -3378,7 +3570,7 @@ IOMobileFramebufferUserClient::externalMethod(uint32_t selector, IOExternalMetho
 			if (ret != kIOReturnSuccess) { fFB->rejectFrame(get32(rec, 0x50), 11, ret); }
 			if (retired) { break; }
 		}
-		ret = fFB->swap(rec);
+		ret = fFB->swap(rec, this);
 		break;
 	}
 	case kSweetFBGetID:
